routinely processed breast carcinomas with determinations of progesterone receptors by a hormone binding assay  Sixty five percent of tumors that stained with this antibody contained progesterone receptors by the biochemical assay  and 98  of tumors that failed to stain lacked the receptors  THE RESULTS OF IMMUNOHISTOCHEMICAL STAINING FOR PROGESTERONE RECEPTOR PROTEIN ARE FOR INVESTIGATIONAL USE ONLY  THEY MUST NOT BE USED FOR CLINICAL PURPOSES  Added by  Dennis Sgroi  M D  Signed On  02 MAY 95 at 15 15 GROSS DESCRIPTION  Received fresh in the Frozen Section Lab   labeled REMOVE_PATIENT_NAME  patient s name and unit number only  is a REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x 0 9cm portion of tan yellow fibroadipose tissue  with needle localizing wire in place  and accompanying specimen roentgenogram  The specimen is inked and sectioned to reveal a single  REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x 0 3cm area of dense tissue surrounded by yellow  soft  lobulated adipose tissue  The suspicious area extends to the ink  and is located in the mid portion of the thickened segment of the wire  The lesion is too small to freeze  and no frozen section is done  Submitted entirely in A through J  with the lesion in A and B  Resident  STAMATINA KAPTAIN  MD     INTRA OPERATIVE DIAGNOSIS     GROSS DIAGNOSIS  LEFT BREAST  REMOVED_DATE CM NODULE SUSPICIOUS FOR CARCINOMA  BUT TOO SMALL TO FREEZE  O R  Resident  JAMES J  TIESINGA  MD O R  Diagnosis by  Eugene Jerome Mark  M D                                                                                  SOURCE CARE UNIT  SURGICAL DAY CARE   ACC 3 REPORTS TO  Charles Mason Ferguson  M D  PART   LEFT BREAST   report_end  , Unchecked.
